Two Tips:

&amp;#x200B;

Get yourself a spanner nut tool from ebay, usually around $10. Just search for "Walkie Talkie Cross Type antenna tool" you will need to remove the retaining nuts around each control knob and antenna connector. Using pliers or some other tool not designed for the task is a pain and tends to rip up the notches in those nuts.

&amp;#x200B;

For removing the diodes I like to use two soldering irons, heat both sides of the SMD diode at the same time and when the solder melts you can just slide the component right off the pads. I usually go back with some flux and desoldering braid to clean up the pads and remove any leftover solder blobs.

This may be related to talker alias. If you are talking on brandmeister and using a openspot or MMDVM  / PiStar hotspot or receiving from a Hytera repeater you may be seeing the talker alias.

&amp;#x200B;

Here is what I think is happening, just a hunch mind you.

&amp;#x200B;

You loaded the contacts but in not exactly the right format. When the local contact is taking priority you see just the radio ID and when you are receiving the talker alias data you see the full contact info.

&amp;#x200B;

On my anytone I use the N0GSG contact manager for digital contact list loading.

[http://n0gsg.com/contact-manager/](http://n0gsg.com/contact-manager/)

&amp;#x200B;

In the anytone CPS go to Tools -&gt; Export Data Conversion File

Open the DCF file in contact manager and click on "Import Contacts from DMR Online Database"

Contact Manager collects records from both the Ham Digital and Radio ID databases to get the most up-to-date information on worldwide DMR users.

Load your contacts, save the DCF and re-import it into the CPS for programing.

