It's the easiest Yaesu to use, but that's not saying much, haha. I have some gripes about it, mainly the fact that with both VFOs active you can't see the memory name on either VFO it just shows the frequency. I can't memorize all those frequencies so it's a pain use in dual VFO/Memory mode. It also has some other UI shortcomings, lots of the settings / functions I use are buried deep in the menus and sometimes split across a few menus.

&amp;#x200B;

However, Yaesu fusion really sounds good and the FT2 is way easier to use than the FT1 or the FT70 for fusion operation, plus the PDN mode when hooked up to a computer has really increased my use of fusion. 

&amp;#x200B;

Despite it's shortcomings it still has great build quality and very good receive sensitivity, if you have a need for fusion the current $299 asking price is pretty great value.

Sure!

&amp;#x200B;

Kenwood D72

My first full featured HT. I used it for everything, lost one at a gas station once and bought another one. Now that I have the D74, it serves as my uplink radio for FM satellites.

&amp;#x200B;

Kenwood D74

Love this radio! It's basically a next generation D72. I picked it up for DStar mainly since I was turned off by the UI of the Icom ID-51. Turns out this radio does so many things well, it's a APRS beast , the bluetooth KISS modem + APRSDroid is a sweet combo. The VFO recorder is super handy as a downlink radio for FM satellites and it's a dream to use on DStar. I also super appreciate that it does simultaneous receive on both VFOs.

&amp;#x200B;

Yaesu FT2D

Not exactly easy to operate but way better then older Yaesu models, I use this mainly for Wires-X / YSF. It's nice to be able to hook this up to a PC and operate it in PDN mode to get onto the Wires-X network without a Wires-X repeater or node nearby. Also, wideband simplex C4FM sounds amazing.

&amp;#x200B;

Yaesu VX1/3

Pocket radios, I keep the VX3 in my EDC so I always have a radio on me, the VX3 has a great receiver. I've used it to monitor marine VHF, air traffic control, check into the net when I've forgotten my regular HT or the battery died. It's nice to have around when you just need to listen to "just one more frequency", lol. The VX1 mostly collects dust nowadays.

&amp;#x200B;

Yaesu FT-65

Picked it up recently for $80, it's such a great price. I wanted something that had a decently loud speaker on it and I wouldn't have to worry if it got trashed. I like the build quality on this radio much better than the Chinese imports and reports by folks who have hooked it up to test equipment had nothing but praise for it's RF performance. I'll take it out to the sand dunes next week while we rip it up in the desert and see how it does.

&amp;#x200B;

Anytone 868/878

I picked this up as DMR upgrade to my TYT MD380 which I passed on to my brother. It started out as a 868 and I firmware upgraded it to a 878 by flashing the MCU directly. It's a great DMR radio despite some Chinese radio annoyances. I really wish it would do simultaneous receive, at least one DMR and one Analog, also the volume won't go down super low, the lowest setting is sometimes too loud when the kiddo is sleeping. 

&amp;#x200B;

Uniden BCD396XT

APCO P25 scanner, bunch of P25 in my area so I use this guy for that. Also it's my general use scanner for public safety, local PD / FD dispatch, local airport, lifeguards, marine VHF etc. I like the "close call" feature that will watch a entire band for a strong signal and lock onto it. It helps find local coms when you are on location.
